**Smallest Correct Fix:**

To fix this issue, you should either remove the use of ThreadLocals or implement a mechanism to clear the ThreadLocal map after each request. This can be achieved by creating a cleanup method that removes the ThreadLocal map for the current thread after the request has been processed. Alternatively, you can use a bounded ThreadLocal implementation that clears the map after a certain number of requests.

```java
public class RequestContext {
    private static final ThreadLocal<Map<String, Object>> STATE =
        ThreadLocal.withInitial(() -> new HashMap<>());

    public static void put(String k, Object v) {
        Map<String, Object> map = STATE.get();
        map.put(k, v);
        // Clear the map after use to avoid memory leaks
        map.clear();
    }
}
```

Note: The above code snippet is an example of how to clear the ThreadLocal map after each request. The actual implementation may vary depending on the specific requirements and constraints of the application.

